          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life</strong><br>\nGustave Courbet was born on June 10, 1819, in Ornans, France. From a young age, he showed a strong interest in art, leading him to pursue a career as a painter. He moved to Paris in 1839 to study at the studio of Steuben and Hesse but soon developed his own style.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Artistic Style</strong><br>\nCourbet is known for his leading role in the Realist movement. Rejecting the romantic painting of his time, he chose subjects that were considered mundane or unidealized. His focus was on painting everyday scenes and people, often depicting the harsh realities of life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Notable Works</strong><br>\nOne of Courbet's most famous works is \"The Stone Breakers,\" which shows laborers breaking rocks by the road. This 1849 painting was a stark departure from the historical and mythological subjects that were popular in the art of his era.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
