          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Painter's Studio</strong><br>\nCourbet's \"The Painter's Studio: A Real Allegory\" is a grand statement of his Realist manifesto. Completed in 1855, this work was self-funded and exhibited when it was rejected by the Paris World's Fair, reflecting his commitment to artistic independence.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Involvement</strong><br>\nCourbet was actively involved in politics and was a member of the socialist movement. He took a prominent role during the Paris Commune of 1871, after which he was arrested and sentenced to six months in prison for his political engagement.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Exile and Death</strong><br>\nFollowing the destruction of the Vendome Column, which Courbet proposed during the Paris Commune, he was held responsible and sentenced to pay for its reconstruction. Unable to pay the hefty fine, he went into self-imposed exile in Switzerland where he died on December 31, 1877.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
